==Cast and characters==
{{Main|List of The Flash characters|l1=List of ''The Flash'' characters|List of Arrowverse cast members|l2=List of ''Arrowverse'' cast members}}
* [[Grant Gustin]] as [[Barry Allen (Arrowverse)|Barry Allen / The Flash]]: <br />A [[Central City (DC Comics)|Central City]] assistant police forensic investigator. Moments after an explosion at the [[S.T.A.R. Labs]] particle accelerator, Barry is struck by lightning in his laboratory and doused by chemicals affected by the accident. When he awakens from a nine-month coma, he has superhuman speed.<ref name="Gustin" /> In September 2013, Grant Gustin was cast in the titular role.<ref name="Gustin" /> [[Andy Mientus]], who would eventually be cast as [[Hartley Rathaway (Arrowverse)|Hartley Rathaway]], also auditioned for the role.<ref name="MientusFlashAudition" /> Gustin began researching the character during the audition process, and reading as many comics as possible. Gustin primarily focused on [[The New 52]] series of comics, because he knew it would be difficult to read everything and he felt the New 52 was the closest to the show's "look and feel".<ref name="AccessHoll">{{Cite web |last1=Lash |first1=Jolie |last2=Ramos |first2=Anthony |date=October 7, 2014 |title=''The Flash'' Q&A: Grant Gustin Talks Taking On Barry Allen, His Amazing Superhero Suit! |url=https://www.accessonline.com/articles/the-flash-qa-grant-gustin-talks-taking-on-barry-allen-his-amazing-superhero-suit-152586 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141223061445/https://www.accesshollywood.com/the-flash-qanda-grant-gustin-talks-taking-on-barry-allen-his-amazing-superhero-suit_article_99383 |archive-date=December 23, 2014 |access-date=March 22, 2022}}</ref>
* [[Candice Patton]] as [[Iris West-Allen (Arrowverse)|Iris West-Allen]]: The daughter of Joe West, sister to Wally West, and eventually Barry Allen's wife, getting engaged in "[[Duet (The Flash)|Duet]]" and marrying during the Arrowverse crossover "[[Crisis on Earth-X]]".<ref name="Patton" /> She initially works at Central City Picture News as a journalist,<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 5, 2013 |title=Iris West "The Flash" Character Description Surfaces Following "Arrow" Ratings Jump |url=https://www.cbr.com/iris-west-the-flash-character-description-surfaces-following-arrow-ratings-jump/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160206144200/https://www.comicbookresources.com/?page=article&id=49531 |archive-date=February 6, 2016 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=[[Comic Book Resources]]}}</ref> and eventually establishes her own newspaper, ''The Central City Citizen''.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |date=January 29, 2019 |title=The Flash recap: Journey through Nora's memories |url=https://ew.com/recap/the-flash-season-5-episode-12/ |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210901054741/https://ew.com/recap/the-flash-season-5-episode-12/ |archive-date=September 1, 2021 |access-date=August 20, 2021}}</ref>
* [[Danielle Panabaker]] as [[Caitlin Snow (Arrowverse)|Caitlin Snow / Killer Frost / Frost / Khione]]:<ref name="Firestorm" /> <br />A highly intelligent bioengineering expert, Caitlin believed her fiancé, [[Ronnie Raymond (Arrowverse)|Ronnie Raymond]],<ref name="Firestorm" /> was killed during the particle accelerator explosion at S.T.A.R. Labs,<ref name="Cosnett/Panabaker" /> until he returned part way through first season. Ronnie and Caitlin get married in the first season finale "Fast Enough".<ref name="FastEnough">{{Cite web |last=Schedeen |first=Jesse |date=May 19, 2015 |title=The Flash: "Fast Enough" Review |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/05/20/the-flash-fast-enough-review |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191014054440/https://uk.ign.com/articles/2015/05/20/the-flash-fast-enough-review |archive-date=October 14, 2019 |access-date=May 21, 2015 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref> She is also a metahuman with cryokinetic abilities known as Killer Frost and later Frost, and later also gains a persona, Khione, that allows her to connect to the natural world.
* [[Rick Cosnett]] as [[Eddie Thawne|Eddie Thawne / "Malcolm Gilmore" / Cobalt Blue]] (season 1; recurring: season 9; guest: seasons 2–3 & 8): <br />A CCPD detective who transferred from Keystone City, who works as Joe's partner and Iris' boyfriend. Although initially seeing the Flash as a menace, he later comes to be a close ally. After learning he is the ancestor of Eobard Thawne–the Reverse-Flash, Eddie commits suicide to wipe him from existence. Years later, Eddie is mysteriously resurrected, and is given a false life and memories as Malcolm Gilmore. When Eddie remembers his real identity and comes to terms with the changes that have occurred since his suicide attempt, including Eobard surviving and Iris marrying Barry, Eddie is chosen as the new avatar for the Negative Speed Force, becoming the speedster Cobalt Blue in the process. He fights team Flash, but eventually stands down and forms a truce with Barry.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=2023-05-25 |title=The Flash Series Finale Recap: An Evil Legion Assembles, a Near-Death, a Birth, a Big Return and New [Spoilers] |url=https://www.tvline.com/lists/the-flash-recap-series-finale-nora-born-caitlin-returns-season-9-episode-13/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230525042021/https://tvline.com/lists/the-flash-recap-series-finale-nora-born-caitlin-returns-season-9-episode-13/ |archive-date=May 25, 2023 |access-date=2023-05-25 |website=[[TVLine]] |language=en-US}}</ref>
* [[Carlos Valdes (actor)|Carlos Valdes]] as [[Cisco Ramon (Arrowverse)|Cisco Ramon / Vibe / Mecha-Vibe]] (seasons 1–7): <br />A mechanical engineering genius, Cisco is the youngest member of the team of scientists at S.T.A.R. Labs and is a former metahuman who had the power to "vibe" a person's location and was able to travel across the multiverse.<ref name="Valdes" /> He leaves the team during the seventh season to move to Star City with his girlfriend Kamila for a job at A.R.G.U.S., but returns to help team Flash one more time in the season finale.
* [[Tom Cavanagh]] as [[Eobard Thawne (Arrowverse)|Eobard Thawne / Reverse-Flash]] (seasons 1–6; recurring: season 8; guest: season 7 & 9) and [[Harrison Wells]] (seasons 1–7; guest: season 9): <br />The mind and money behind the [[S.T.A.R. Labs]]' [[Particle Accelerator]] who becomes a pariah after his device implodes and creates a large number of [[metahuman]]s in Central City.<ref name="Cavanagh" /> Wells mentors Barry and helps to increase his speed. He is eventually revealed to be Eddie Thawne's time-traveling descendant and the Flash's archenemy from the future, Eobard Thawne–the Reverse-Flash. Eobard murdered the real Wells and assumed his identity.<ref name="WellsThawne" /> In subsequent seasons, Cavanagh also portrays various doppelgängers of Wells from [[Parallel universe (fiction)|alternate realities]], including the sarcastic but brilliant "Harry" from [[Earth-2 (Arrowverse)|Earth-2]]; sci-fi novelist "H.R." from [[Earth-19 (Arrowverse)|Earth-19]]; keen detective "Sherloque" from [[Earth-221]]; and adventurous geologist "Nash".<ref name="Nash">{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=October 20, 2019 |title=The Flash Season 6: Meet Nash Wells, 'A Man of Adventure'... With a Secret |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-flash-season-6-nash-wells-adventurer-with-secret-1115399/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191020230526/https://tvline.com/2019/10/20/the-flash-season-6-nash-wells-adventurer-with-secret/ |archive-date=October 20, 2019 |access-date=October 20, 2019 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>
* [[Jesse L. Martin]] as [[Joe West (Arrowverse)|Joe West]] (seasons 1–8; recurring: season 9):<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=April 13, 2022 |title=The Flash's Jesse L. Martin Steps Down as Series Regular, to Star in NBC Pilot |url=https://www.tvline.com/casting-news/the-flash-jesse-l-martin-leaving-season-9-series-regular-joe-west-1234820143/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220413172558/https://tvline.com/2022/04/13/the-flash-jesse-l-martin-leaving-season-9-series-regular-joe-west/ |archive-date=April 13, 2022 |access-date=April 13, 2022 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> <br />A police detective who acts as a surrogate father to Barry, taking him into his home after Barry's mother is murdered and his father is unjustly imprisoned for the crime. Joe is the father of Iris<ref name="S1LogLine" /><ref name="Martin" /> and Wally.<ref name="WallyJoeSon" /> He also now has a daughter with his fiancée, district attorney Cecille Horton, named Jenna West. Martin took a medical leave of absence from the show during season five after suffering a back injury.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |title=Jesse L. Martin is taking a medical leave of absence from The Flash |url=https://ew.com/tv/2018/10/31/jesse-l-martin-medical-leave-of-absence-the-flash/ |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181106230730/https://ew.com/tv/2018/10/31/jesse-l-martin-medical-leave-of-absence-the-flash/ |archive-date=November 6, 2018 |access-date=November 8, 2018}}</ref>
* [[Keiynan Lonsdale]] as [[Wally West (Arrowverse)|Wally West / Kid Flash]] (seasons 2–4; guest: seasons 5–6 & 9): <br />The son of Joe and brother of Iris, born without their knowledge after his mother left Central City.<ref name="WallyJoeSon">{{Cite web |last=Goldman |first=Eric |date=October 28, 2015 |title=The Flash Producer Talks Wally West, King Shark and the Next Arrow Crossover |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/10/28/the-flash-producer-talks-wally-west-king-shark-and-the-next-arrow-crossover |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151030042716/https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/10/28/the-flash-producer-talks-wally-west-king-shark-and-the-next-arrow-crossover |archive-date=October 30, 2015 |access-date=October 28, 2015 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref> The producers disliked the introduction of relatives of characters that were never previously mentioned, feeling it was "weird", and opted instead to introduce Wally as unknown even to his relatives. They also chose to make him Iris' brother, a departure from the comics, where he was her nephew.<ref name="WallyJoeSon" /> Explaining Lonsdale's casting, Kreisberg said, "Just like when we met Grant [Gustin] for the first time, we instantly knew Keiynan embodied all the heart and courage of a hero."<ref name="WallyFlashS2">{{Cite news |last=Goldman |first=Eric |date=August 5, 2015 |title=The Flash: Wally West Cast for Season 2 |publisher=[[IGN]] |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/08/05/the-flash-wally-west-cast-for-season-2 |url-status=live |access-date=August 5, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150805215933/https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/08/05/the-flash-wally-west-cast-for-season-2 |archive-date=August 5, 2015}}</ref> After Flashpoint, Wally eventually gets speed powers from [[Julian Albert|Doctor Alchemy]] and becomes Kid Flash. In season four, he leaves Central City to break out of Barry's shadow, and during that time he joins the Legends. He returns home briefly following the birth of half-sister Jenna West, and to confront Barry when problems with the Speed Force surface.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gelman |first=Vlada |date=July 12, 2016 |title=The Flash First Look: Meet Kid Flash |url=https://tvline.com/2016/07/12/wally-west-kid-flash-photo-the-flash-season-3-spoilers/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160713155453/https://tvline.com/2016/07/12/wally-west-kid-flash-photo-the-flash-season-3-spoilers/ |archive-date=July 13, 2016 |access-date=July 12, 2016 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>
* [[Neil Sandilands]] as [[Clifford DeVoe|Clifford DeVoe / The Thinker]] (season 4):<br />A metahuman with superhuman intelligence. Originally a mild-mannered professor who dreamed of expanding the way people think, DeVoe allows his power to go to his head and seeks to fix all that he deems wrong with humanity as the Thinker; his methods include creating new metahumans to hunt and steal their powers, and framing Barry for murder. His control-freak, power-hungry ways eventually turn his wife Marlize against him, and she eventually helps team Flash wipe him from existence.
* [[Hartley Sawyer]] as [[Ralph Dibny (Arrowverse)|Ralph Dibny / Elongated Man]] (seasons 5–6; recurring: season 4): <br />A private investigator, formerly a corrupt CCPD cop, Dibny is a metahuman with superpowers including body elasticity and malleability. He becomes a member of Team Flash.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pedersen |first=Erik |date=June 15, 2018 |title='The Flash': Hartley Sawyer Upped To Series Regular On The CW Superhero Drama |url=https://deadline.com/2018/06/the-flash-season-5-hartley-sawyer-elongated-man-series-regular-the-cw-1202411128/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180615183307/https://deadline.com/2018/06/the-flash-season-5-hartley-sawyer-elongated-man-series-regular-the-cw-1202411128/ |archive-date=June 15, 2018 |access-date=June 16, 2018 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> After defeating Mirror Monarch, Dibny left Central City with [[Sue Dearbon (Arrowverse)|Sue Dearbon]] to hunt down other criminal organizations. This was written into the show following Sawyer's firing from the series in June 2020 prior to the start of production of the seventh season due to racist, misogynistic tweets he made in the past resurfacing.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=June 8, 2020 |title='The Flash': Hartley Sawyer Fired After Racist, Misogynist Tweets Resurface |url=https://deadline.com/2020/06/the-flash-hartley-sawyer-fired-racist-misogynist-social-media-1202953632/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200608182306/https://deadline.com/2020/06/the-flash-hartley-sawyer-fired-racist-misogynist-social-media-1202953632/ |archive-date=June 8, 2020 |access-date=June 8, 2020 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>
* [[Danielle Nicolet]] as [[Cecile Horton (Arrowverse)|Cecile Horton / Virtue]] (seasons 5–9; recurring: seasons 3–4; guest: season 1): <br />A district attorney who becomes Joe West's girlfriend and the mother of their daughter Jenna West. While pregnant, she experiences temporary [[telepathy|telepathic]] metahuman superpowers.<ref name="Nicolet">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=June 13, 2018 |title='The Flash': Danielle Nicolet Upped To Series Regular For Season 5 Of CW Drama |url=https://deadline.com/2018/06/the-flash-danielle-nicolet-promoted-series-regular-1202409871/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614164021/https://deadline.com/2018/06/the-flash-danielle-nicolet-promoted-series-regular-1202409871/ |archive-date=June 14, 2018 |access-date=June 14, 2018 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> After her pregnancy, she develops new metahuman abilities of empathy.
* [[Jessica Parker Kennedy]] as [[Nora West-Allen|Nora West-Allen / XS]] (season 5; recurring: seasons 4 & 7–8; guest: season 9): <br />A mysterious girl with super-speed powers, who is eventually revealed to be Barry and Iris' future daughter. She arrives in the future with the help of Eobard Thawne to help take on Cicada, a metahuman who hates and aims to exterminate all other metas. She eventually is erased from existence after the source of Cicada's powers was destroyed. A different version of Nora appears starting with the seventh season, assisting her father in the Godspeed war and later Thawne's re-emergence.
* [[Chris Klein (actor)|Chris Klein]] as [[Orlin Dwyer|Orlin Dwyer / Cicada]] (season 5): <br />A metahuman who hates and aims to exterminate all other metas. Developing his powers and views after separate meta incidents that also kill his sister and injure his niece Grace, Cicada initially aims to kill all metahumans, including eventually himself. He eventually gives this and his powers up after learning of Grace's meta status, but is later killed by a future version of Grace who had embraced his old views, although a part of him survives long enough to help avert this future by convincing present-day Grace.
* [[LaMonica Garrett]] as [[Mar Novu (Arrowverse)|Mar Novu / Monitor]] (season 6; guest: season 5): a multiversal being testing different Earths in the multiverse in preparation for an impending "[[Crisis on Infinite Earths (Arrowverse)|crisis]]". He made his first appearance in the Arrowverse crossover "[[Elseworlds (Arrowverse)|Elseworlds]]".
** Garrett also portrays [[Anti-Monitor (Arrowverse)|Mobius / Anti-Monitor]] (season 6), the Monitor's polar opposite, an evil being dedicated to ending the multiverse.
* [[Efrat Dor]] as [[Eva McCulloch| Eva McCulloch / Mirror Monarch]] (seasons 6–7): <br />A quantum engineer and co-founder of McCulloch Technologies who was seemingly trapped in the [[Mirrorverse]] for six years after a particle accelerator explosion.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |date=November 21, 2019 |title=The Flash casts Mayans M.C. actress in role that 'pushes the boundaries of sanity' |url=https://ew.com/tv/2019/11/21/the-flash-efrat-dor-eva-mcculloch-iris/ |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191123051619/https://ew.com/tv/2019/11/21/the-flash-efrat-dor-eva-mcculloch-iris/ |archive-date=November 23, 2019 |access-date=November 22, 2019}}</ref> After discovering her husband has perverted her company and technology to create the criminal organization Black Hole, she seeks to escape and get revenge, kidnapping Iris, Kamila, and Chief Singh and replacing them with mirror duplicates in order to secure the resources to escape safely. After doing so, she kills her husband, destroys Black Hole's resources and takes back her company. Shortly afterwards, it is revealed that the present Eva is actually a mirror copy and the real Eva perished in the particle accelerator explosion. Upon learning the truth, she tries to replace everyone in Central City with duplicates to bring them down to her level. Flash and Iris persuade her to give up on her plans and after destroying the duplicates with their help and returning all the people, she leaves for the Mirrorverse again to start anew.
* [[Kayla Compton]] as [[Allegra Garcia]] (seasons 7–9; recurring: season 6): <br />A young metahuman with electromagnetic abilities and aspiring reporter.
* Brandon McKnight as [[Chester P. Runk (Arrowverse)|Chester P. Runk]] (seasons 7–9; guest: season 6): <br />A scientist who was rescued by Team Flash from his own experiment and now aids them while rebuilding his life.
* [[Jon Cor]] as [[Mark Blaine|Mark Blaine / Chillblaine]] (season 9; recurring: season 7–8): <br />A former cryogenicist-turned-criminal who becomes a rival and love interest for Frost.<ref name="JonCor">{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=October 24, 2022 |title='The Flash': Jon Cor Upped To Series Regular For Season 9 Of CW Series |url=https://deadline.com/2022/10/the-flash-jon-cor-promoted-series-regular-season-9-cw-series-mark-blaine-1235152370/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221024222414/https://deadline.com/2022/10/the-flash-jon-cor-promoted-series-regular-season-9-cw-series-mark-blaine-1235152370/ |archive-date=October 24, 2022 |access-date=October 24, 2022 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>

==Production==
===Development===
On July 30, 2013, it was announced that ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' co-creators [[Greg Berlanti]] and [[Andrew Kreisberg]], ''Arrow'' pilot director [[David Nutter]], and DC Comics CCO [[Geoff Johns]] would develop a television series based on the Flash for [[The CW]], and it would detail Barry Allen's origin.<ref name="DeadlineAnnouncement" /> Kreisberg revealed after the announcement that Allen would first appear as a recurring character on ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' in three episodes of season two—all written by Berlanti, Kreisberg and Johns—and the last of the episodes would act as a [[backdoor pilot]] for the new show. Kreisberg added that Allen would be a forensic scientist and the introduction of his superpowers, as well as the reactions to this, will be very human and grounded. Johns stated that the character of the Flash in the show would resemble his comic book counterpart, complete with his trademark red costume, and not be a poor imitation. Kreisberg elaborated: "No sweat suits or strange code names; he ''will be'' The Flash." While researching the best way to depict the Flash's lightning speed, Johns stated it would not just be the standard "blurring around".<ref name="THRJuly2013" />

Barry ultimately appeared twice in ''Arrow''{{'s}} second season, with the planned backdoor pilot cancelled in favor of a traditional pilot by The CW executives, who had been impressed by early cuts of Barry's first two episodes on ''Arrow''. This allowed the creative team to flesh out Barry's story and his world on a bigger budget, as opposed to a backdoor pilot's constraint of incorporating characters from the parent show. The pilot was officially ordered on January 29, 2014, and was written by Berlanti, Kreisberg, and Johns, and directed by Nutter.<ref name="DeadlinePilot" /><ref name="PilotOrdered" /> On May 8, 2014, ''The Flash'' was officially picked up as a series, with an initial order of 13 episodes.<ref name="PickUp" /> Three more scripts were ordered in September 2014 following a positive response to newly completed episodes by executives,<ref name="Deadline3">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=September 23, 2014 |title=CW's 'The Flash' & 'Jane The Virgin' Get Backup Script Orders Ahead Of Premieres |url=https://deadline.com/2014/09/the-flash-jane-the-virgin-additional-scripts-cw-838768/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140925082725/https://deadline.com/2014/09/the-flash-jane-the-virgin-additional-scripts-cw-838768/ |archive-date=September 25, 2014 |access-date=September 24, 2014 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> while a back ten was ordered the next month for a full 23-episode season.<ref name="S1SeasonOrder" />

With the commencement of production on the series' second season, former ''Arrow'' and ''[[Ugly Betty]]'' writer [[Gabrielle Stanton]] was promoted to executive producer and showrunner; after having served as consulting producer and writer on the first season's finale "Fast Enough".<ref name="Stanton1">{{Cite web |last=Webb Mitovich |first=Matt |date=August 17, 2015 |title=Matt's Inside Line: Scoop on Once, Arrow, Castle, The Flash, Scandal, Gotham, Reign, Haven and More |url=https://www.tvline.com/casting-news/once-upon-a-time-season-5-belle-merida-spoilers-634781/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150818064756/https://tvline.com/2015/08/17/once-upon-a-time-season-5-belle-merida-spoilers/ |archive-date=August 18, 2015 |access-date=August 18, 2015 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> However, it was later reported that Kreisberg would be returning to sole showrunner duties at an unspecified time.<ref name="THRdeal">{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=November 3, 2013 |title='The Flash' Boss Andrew Kreisberg Inks Overall Deal With Warner Bros. TV (Exclusive) |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/andrew-kreisberg-deal-warner-bros-831387 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151106004350/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/andrew-kreisberg-deal-warner-bros-831387 |archive-date=November 6, 2015 |access-date=November 3, 2014 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> That time was later proved to be at the start of 2016, "Potential Energy", when Stanton was no longer credited as being involved with the show. In May 2017, it was reported that [[Aaron and Todd Helbing|Aaron Helbing]] would be departing the series. Helbing had served as a writer since the first season, and as co-showrunner, along with his brother [[Aaron and Todd Helbing|Todd]] and Kreisberg, since the second.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Burlingame |first=Russ |date=May 22, 2017 |title=Aaron Helbing Off the Flash in Season 4 |url=https://comicbook.com/dc/2017/05/22/aaron-helbing-off-the-flash-in-season-4/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170630054053/https://comicbook.com/dc/2017/05/22/aaron-helbing-off-the-flash-in-season-4 |archive-date=June 30, 2017 |access-date=July 4, 2017 |website=[[ComicBook.com]]}}</ref> In 2019, Todd departed as showrunner, and Eric Wallace, who had been co-executive producer since the fourth season, was promoted to the sole showrunner, effective from season six.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=March 12, 2019 |title='The Flash' Changing Showrunners for Season 6 (Exclusive) |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/flash-changing-showrunners-season-6-1193971/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220701072229/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/flash-changing-showrunners-season-6-1193971/ |archive-date=July 1, 2022 |access-date=July 1, 2022 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref>

On April 2, 2018, The CW renewed the series for a [[The Flash season 5|fifth season]], which premiered on October 9, 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=April 2, 2018 |title='Riverdale,' 'Flash,' 'Supernatural' Among 10 CW Renewals |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/riverdale-flash-supernatural-10-cw-renewals-1098997 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180402195748/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/riverdale-flash-supernatural-10-cw-renewals-1098997 |archive-date=April 2, 2018 |access-date=April 2, 2018 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref><ref name="S5PremiereDate">{{Cite web |last=Pedersen |first=Erik |date=June 20, 2018 |title=The CW Sets Fall Premiere Dates, Including Sunday Lineup & Three Series Debuts |url=https://deadline.com/2018/06/the-cw-fall-2018-premiere-dates-charmed-all-american-legacies-1202414400/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180621004604/https://deadline.com/2018/06/the-cw-fall-2018-premiere-dates-charmed-all-american-legacies-1202414400/ |archive-date=June 21, 2018 |access-date=June 20, 2018 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> On January 31, 2019, The CW renewed the series for a sixth season,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=January 31, 2019 |title=CW Renews 'The Flash,' 'Charmed,' 'Riverdale,' 'Supernatural,' 6 More |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/cw-renews-flash-charmed-riverdale-supernatural-6-more-1181714 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190202015411/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/cw-renews-flash-charmed-riverdale-supernatural-6-more-1181714 |archive-date=February 2, 2019 |access-date=January 31, 2019 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> which premiered on October 8, 2019.<ref name="S6PremiereDate">{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=June 17, 2019 |title=The CW Sets Fall Premiere Dates: 'Batwoman', 'Supergirl', 'The Flash', 'Nancy Drew', More |url=https://deadline.com/2019/06/the-cw-sets-fall-premiere-dates-batwoman-supergirl-the-flash-nancy-drew-1202633821/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190618053243/https://deadline.com/2019/06/the-cw-sets-fall-premiere-dates-batwoman-supergirl-the-flash-nancy-drew-1202633821/ |archive-date=June 18, 2019 |access-date=June 17, 2019 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> On January 7, 2020, the series was renewed for a seventh season, which premiered on March 2, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ausiello |first=Michael |date=January 7, 2020 |title=CW Renews Riverdale, Batwoman, Roswell, Black Lightning Plus 9 Others |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-cw-renewed-shows-2020-list-riverdale-flash-1142188/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200108032431/https://tvline.com/2020/01/07/the-cw-renewed-shows-2020-list-riverdale-flash/ |archive-date=January 8, 2020 |access-date=January 7, 2020 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> In April 2020, Gustin, who had been contracted for seven seasons, said there had been discussions about renewing the series through a ninth season, but those were stalled due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=April 30, 2020 |title=Flash's Grant Gustin: Seasons 8 and 9 Talks Were Interrupted by Pandemic |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-flash-grant-gustin-season-8-and-9-renewal-talks-1191821/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220129070733/https://tvline.com/2020/04/30/the-flash-grant-gustin-season-8-and-9-renewal-talks/ |archive-date=January 29, 2022 |access-date=July 23, 2020 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> On February 3, 2021, the series was renewed for an eighth season which premiered on November 16, 2021.<ref name="S8Renewal">{{Cite web |last=White |first=Peter |date=February 3, 2021 |title=The CW Renews 12 Series Including Freshman Drama 'Walker' |url=https://deadline.com/2021/02/the-cw-major-renewal-move-new-seasons-12-shows-walker-1234686788/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210203211446/https://deadline.com/2021/02/the-cw-major-renewal-move-new-seasons-12-shows-walker-1234686788/ |archive-date=February 3, 2021 |access-date=February 3, 2021 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref name="S8Premiere">{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=June 15, 2021 |title=The CW Sets Fall Premiere Dates for ''The Flash'', ''Walker'', ''4400'' and 11 Others |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-cw-fall-premiere-dates-the-flash-walker-riverdale-1234683482/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210616040119/https://tvline.com/2021/06/15/the-cw-fall-premiere-dates-the-flash-walker-riverdale/ |archive-date=June 16, 2021 |access-date=June 15, 2021 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> In late January 2022, Gustin was reported to be renewing his contract for a ninth season.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=January 28, 2022 |title='The Flash': Grant Gustin Nears New Deal, Paving Way For Season 9 Renewal At CW |url=https://deadline.com/2022/01/the-flash-grant-gustin-new-deal-season-9-renewal-cw-barry-allen-1234922147/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220201192925/https://deadline.com/2022/01/the-flash-grant-gustin-new-deal-season-9-renewal-cw-barry-allen-1234922147/ |archive-date=February 1, 2022 |access-date=January 29, 2022 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> On March 22, 2022, The CW renewed the series for a ninth season.<ref>{{Cite web |last=White |first=Peter |date=March 22, 2022 |title=The CW Renews Seven Scripted Series: 'The Flash', 'Kung Fu', 'All American', 'Nancy Drew', 'Superman & Lois', 'Walker' & 'Riverdale' Returning |url=https://deadline.com/2022/03/the-cw-renewals-the-flash-nancy-drew-kung-fu-1234984336/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322220925/https://deadline.com/2022/03/the-cw-renewals-the-flash-nancy-drew-kung-fu-1234984336/ |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> On August 1, 2022, it was announced that the series will be concluding with its upcoming ninth season, and that the season would receive a 13-episode order.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=August 1, 2022 |title='The Flash' To End With Season 9 On the CW, Final Episode Count Revealed |url=https://deadline.com/2022/08/the-flash-end-season-9-the-cw-final-episode-count-rgrant-gustin-1235082436/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220801181624/https://deadline.com/2022/08/the-flash-end-season-9-the-cw-final-episode-count-rgrant-gustin-1235082436/ |archive-date=August 1, 2022 |access-date=August 1, 2022 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> The season premiered on February 8, 2023,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Iannucci |first=Rebecca |date=December 5, 2022 |title=''The Flash''{{'}}s Final Season Set for February Premiere at The CW |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-flash-final-season-9-release-date-cw-1234903724/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221205200126/https://tvline.com/2022/12/05/the-flash-final-season-9-release-date-cw/ |archive-date=December 5, 2022 |access-date=December 5, 2022 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> with the finale airing on May 24 of the same year.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Behbakht |first=Andy |date=2023-03-04 |title=The Flash's Season 9 Finale Release Date Has Now Been Confirmed |url=https://screenrant.com/the-flash-series-finale-final-episode-release-date/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230326035610/https://screenrant.com/the-flash-series-finale-final-episode-release-date/ |archive-date=March 26, 2023 |access-date=2023-03-26 |website=[[Screen Rant]] |language=en}}</ref>

===Design===
The costume was designed by [[Colleen Atwood]], who also designed the costumes for ''Arrow''.<ref name="Costume" /> It features a burgundy color scheme, a masked helmet, and gold accents throughout,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Orange |first=B. Alan |date=February 28, 2014 |title='The Flash': First Photo with Grant Gustin in Costume! |url=https://movieweb.com/the-flash-first-photo-with-grant-gustin-in-costume/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200626123735/https://movieweb.com/the-flash-first-photo-with-grant-gustin-in-costume/ |archive-date=June 26, 2020 |access-date=June 23, 2020 |website=Movieweb}}</ref> and went through multiple adjustments from the moment it was placed in computer renderings to the day of filming the pilot.<ref name="AccessHoll" /> Primarily made of leather, the suit contains areas with a stretchable material to allow Gustin room to bend. According to Atwood "It was all about a costume that could sell speed, Grant [Gustin] was continually moving in the suit, so it had to be designed to make that all happen visually and functionally."<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 31, 2015 |title=How Superhero Costumes are Made |work=[[Fashionista (website)|Fashionista]] |url=https://fashionista.com/2015/03/superhero-costume-design |url-status=live |access-date=September 22, 2015 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20150402165807/http://fashionista.com/2015/03/superhero-costume-design |archive-date=April 2, 2015}}</ref> It initially took Gustin approximately 40 minutes to get into his costume, as the first cowl was prosthetic and had to be zipped and glued to his face. This was cut down to approximately 15 minutes by episode eight, when designers were able to develop a new cowl that easily slid over Gustin's face and locked into place.<ref name="AccessHoll" /> Maya Mani replaced Atwood as the costume designer for the second season and made slight changes to the Flash costume, such as changing the color of his crest from yellow to white, being faithful to the Flash costume from the comics. This same costume was used for the entirety of the third season.<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 28, 2015 |title=Costume secrets from the upcoming seasons of 'Reign,' 'The Good Wife' and more |url=https://fashionista.com/2015/09/fall-tv-costume-design-october |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20181202065933/https://fashionista.com/2015/09/fall-tv-costume-design-october |archive-date=December 2, 2018 |access-date=September 29, 2015 |work=[[Fashionista (website)|Fashionista]]}}</ref>

The main costume was redesigned for the fourth season. The new costume had a brighter color scheme, more gold accents throughout, gold earpieces, and lightning bolts on the arms and legs. The suit also featured a gold lightning belt. The costume was yet again redesigned for the shows fifth season, with the cowl's chinstrap and the suits' gold accents being removed.

===Filming===
Production on the pilot began in March 2014, with filming taking place in [[Vancouver]], [[British Columbia]];<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 2014 |title=Flash Running After Villains in Vancouver (Photos & Videos) |url=https://www.vancitybuzz.com/2014/03/flash-runs-past-vancouver-art-gallery/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140621050930/https://www.vancitybuzz.com/2014/03/flash-runs-past-vancouver-art-gallery/ |archive-date=June 21, 2014 |access-date=June 25, 2014 |publisher=Vancity Buzz}}</ref> additional filming for the series takes place in [[Portland, Oregon]].<ref name="PortlandFilming" /> On how action sequences are shot for the series, compared to ''Arrow'', Gustin said, "When [''Arrow''] shoot[s] action sequences, pretty much what you see is what you get and they're really doing everything. We do a lot of plate shots that are empty shots of the area we're going to be in and then they're putting us in later in [[Post-production|post]]. I do a lot of the fighting. I don't have to do it full speed and then they ramp it up and a lot of people have to freeze and I keep moving. Then I have to clear frame and step back into frame. It's really tedious stuff that we have to do. On theirs, they learn fight choreography and they shoot it from the perfect angles and what you see is what you get."<ref name="FightShooting" /> Production on the third season began in early July 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 7, 2016 |title=The Flash Season 3 Started Filming |url=https://www.cosmicbooknews.com/content/flash-season-3-started-filming |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160710052206/https://www.cosmicbooknews.com/content/flash-season-3-started-filming |archive-date=July 10, 2016 |access-date=July 9, 2016 |publisher=Cosmic Book News}}</ref> On March 13, 2020, production on the sixth season was shut down due to the COVID-19 pandemic.<ref name="FilmingSuspended">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=March 13, 2020 |title='The Flash' Shuts Down Production Over Coronavirus |url=https://deadline.com/2020/03/the-flash-shuts-down-production-coronavirus-1202882446/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200313190957/https://deadline.com/2020/03/the-flash-shuts-down-production-coronavirus-1202882446/ |archive-date=March 13, 2020 |access-date=March 13, 2020 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> Season seven began filming in October 2020 and concluded on May 19, 2021.<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 4, 2020 |title=DGC BC Production List |work=[[Directors Guild of Canada]] |url=https://www.dgc.ca/assets/Uploads/BritishColumbia/AvailsProductionLists/Documents/Production-List.pdf |url-status=live |access-date=September 4, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200828222501/https://www.dgc.ca/assets/Uploads/BritishColumbia/AvailsProductionLists/Documents/Production-List.pdf |archive-date=August 28, 2020}}</ref>

===Music===
''Arrow'' composer [[Blake Neely]] is the primary composer of the series, and was first hired in April 2014 to score the pilot.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 10, 2014 |title=Blake Neely to Score The CW's 'The Flash' |url=https://filmmusicreporter.com/2014/04/10/blake-neely-to-score-the-cws-the-flash/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150925110912/https://filmmusicreporter.com/2014/04/10/blake-neely-to-score-the-cws-the-flash/ |archive-date=September 25, 2015 |access-date=August 5, 2015 |publisher=Film Music Reporter}}</ref><ref name="Neely" /> He had previously composed a theme for Barry Allen which was featured in ''Arrow''{{'}}s [[Arrow season 2|season two]] episodes "[[The Scientist (Arrow)|The Scientist]]" and "[[Three Ghosts]]". The theme was titled "The Scientist" when it was released on the [[Arrow (TV series)#Music|''Arrow: Season 2'' soundtrack]]. According to Neely, "It had to be different [from ''Arrow'']... but it also couldn't be so different that it couldn't fit in the ''Arrow'' universe,... it had to be in a style that could hold hands with ''Arrow''."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Neely |first=Blake |date=September 4, 2015 |title=CBR TV: Super Composer Finds "Flash" & "Arrow" Inspiration for "Legends," Makes "Supergirl" Fun |url=https://www.cbr.com/cbr-tv-super-composer-finds-flash-arrow-inspiration-for-legends-makes-supergirl-fun/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220212122932/https://www.cbr.com/cbr-tv-super-composer-finds-flash-arrow-inspiration-for-legends-makes-supergirl-fun/ |archive-date=February 12, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |publisher=[[Comic Book Resources]]}}</ref> On December 18, 2014, WaterTower Music released a selection of music from ''The Flash''/''Arrow'' crossover episodes, as well as two bonus tracks from their respective 2014 midseason finales.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash vs. Arrow: Music Selections from the Epic 2-Night Event |url=http://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=theflashvsarrow |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141220000519/https://www.watertower-music.com/releases_spotlight.php?search=WTM39625 |archive-date=December 20, 2014 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> The first season, two-disc soundtrack was released on October 16, 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 12, 2015 |title=La-La Land Records to Release Soundtrack Album for The CW's 'The Flash' |url=https://filmmusicreporter.com/2015/08/12/la-la-land-records-to-release-soundtrack-album-for-the-cws-the-flash/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150925104507/https://filmmusicreporter.com/2015/08/12/la-la-land-records-to-release-soundtrack-album-for-the-cws-the-flash/ |archive-date=September 25, 2015 |access-date=September 14, 2015 |publisher=Film Music Reporter}}</ref> The second season's soundtrack was released digitally on July 22, 2016,<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Original Television Soundtrack – Season 2 |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=flash2 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160819002607/https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=flash2 |archive-date=August 19, 2016 |access-date=August 3, 2016 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> and in CD format on July 26, 2016.<ref>{{Cite web |title=THE FLASH – SEASON 2 |url=https://lalalandrecords.com/flash-the-season-2-limited-edition/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211118055810/https://lalalandrecords.com/flash-the-season-2-limited-edition/ |archive-date=November 18, 2021 |access-date=November 18, 2021 |website=La-La Land Records}}</ref> Season 3 saw two soundtrack releases. The first was for the episode "[[Duet (The Flash)|Duet]]", which featured six songs from the episode and a guitar version of the last song "Running Home to You". It was released on March 21, 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash – Music From The Special Episode: Duet |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=flashduet |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210615140221/https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=flashduet |archive-date=June 15, 2021 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> The overall soundtrack was released on October 10, 2017.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Season 3 (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=flash3 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050940/https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=flash3 |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> The score of the "[[Crisis on Earth-X]]" crossover released on June 15, 2018,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Crisis On Earth-X (Original Television Score) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=crisisonearthx |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180830005336/http://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=crisisonearthx |archive-date=August 30, 2018 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> and the season four soundtrack was released on March 15, 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Season 4 (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=flash4 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050938/https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=flash4 |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> The soundtracks for seasons five and six, and of the crossovers "[[Elseworlds (Arrowverse)|Elseworlds]]"<ref>{{Cite web |title=Elseworlds (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=Elseworlds |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050939/https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=Elseworlds |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> and "[[Crisis on Infinite Earths (Arrowverse)|Crisis on Infinite Earths]]",<ref>{{Cite web |title=Crisis on Infinite Earths (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=CrisisInfinite |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050939/https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=CrisisInfinite |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> were not released until February 26 and March 5, 2021, ahead of the seventh season's premiere.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Season 5 (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=Flash5 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050940/https://www.watertower-music.com/dccomics/release.php?title=Flash5 |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Season 6 (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=TheFlash6 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050939/https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=TheFlash6 |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> A soundtrack for the five-episode "Armageddon" event that began the show's eighth season was released on March 4, 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Armageddon (Original Television Soundtrack) |url=https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=FlashArmageddon |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220322050938/https://www.watertower-music.com/releases/release.php?title=FlashArmageddon |archive-date=March 22, 2022 |access-date=March 22, 2022 |website=WaterTower Music}}</ref> A soundtrack for the last three seasons was released on May 26, 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Flash: Seasons 7–9 Soundtrack (2014) |url=https://www.soundtrack.net/album/the-flash-seasons-7-9/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230527033134/https://www.soundtrack.net/album/the-flash-seasons-7-9/ |archive-date=May 27, 2023 |access-date=2023-05-27 |website=www.soundtrack.net}}</ref>

===Sound design===
The sound design for the series is handled by Mark Camperell. The sound effect for Barry is made up of elements of thunder, electricity, jets, fireballs, and various custom whooshes and impacts. Speaking about designing the sound for the Flash's ability, Mark says: "My approach for the sounds of the Flash's ability was to editorially treat him like a really aggressively driven hot rod. This doesn't mean that I used car sounds for him, though. What I mean is that when thinking about how to edit his sounds, I thought about it like cutting a car chase."<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 5, 2015 |title=Sounds for Superheroes: Behind the Supersonic Sound Design for The Flash |url=https://www.asoundeffect.com/the-flash-sound-design/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151006181824/https://www.asoundeffect.com/the-flash-sound-design/ |archive-date=October 6, 2015 |access-date=October 6, 2015 |publisher=Asoundeffect.com}}</ref>

==Release==
===Broadcast===
<!--Do not add channels to this list. We are not a TV guide. If you do so, it will be reverted.-->
''The Flash'' was screened at the Warner Bros. Television and DC Entertainment panel at [[San Diego Comic-Con]] in July 2014.<ref name="PilotComicCon" /> The series officially premiered on [[The CW]] on October 7, 2014, during the [[2014–15 United States network television schedule|2014–15 television season]]<ref name="premiere">{{Cite web |last=Bibel |first=Sara |date=June 25, 2014 |title=The CW Announces Fall Premiere Dates |url=https://tvbythenumbers.zap2it.com/2014/06/25/the-cw-announces-fall-premiere-dates/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714161634/https://tvbythenumbers.zap2it.com/2014/06/25/the-cw-announces-fall-premiere-dates/ |archive-date=July 14, 2014 |access-date=June 25, 2014 |website=[[TV by the Numbers]]}}</ref> and also premiered in Canada on the same night.<ref name="cashowstart">{{Cite web |date=July 29, 2014 |title=CTV Fall 2014 lineup premiere dates announced |url=https://www.ctv.ca/thectvinsider/articles/news/ctv_premieres.aspx/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006073414/https://www.ctv.ca/thectvinsider/articles/news/ctv_premieres.aspx/ |archive-date=October 6, 2014 |access-date=September 30, 2014 |publisher=CTV}}</ref> The second episode was screened at [[New York Comic Con]] on October 9, 2014, as a way to repay the viewers that watched the series' premiere episode.<ref name="Ep2ComicConScreen" /> The series premiered in the United Kingdom and Ireland on October 28, 2014,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tidesw |first=Martin |date=October 8, 2014 |title=When is The Flash UK premiere? |work=The Sentinel (Staffordshire) |url=https://www.stokesentinel.co.uk/Flash-UK-premiere/story-23060198-detail/story.html |url-status=dead |access-date=November 3, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141220084804/https://www.stokesentinel.co.uk/Flash-UK-premiere/story-23060198-detail/story.html |archive-date=December 20, 2014}}</ref> and in Australia on December 3, 2014.<ref name="aushowstart">{{Cite web |date=November 10, 2014 |title=Jane the Virgin and The Flash: Premiere on Fox8 |url=https://www.foxtel.com.au/whats-on/foxtel-insider/jane-the-virgin-and-the-flash-premiere-on-fox8-239971.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141224194710/https://www.foxtel.com.au/whats-on/foxtel-insider/jane-the-virgin-and-the-flash-premiere-on-fox8-239971.htm |archive-date=December 24, 2014 |access-date=January 5, 2015 |publisher=[[Foxtel]]}}</ref>

=== Home media ===
The complete first season was first released on [[Blu-ray]] and [[DVD]] in [[DVD region code#1|Region 1]] on September 22, 2015,<ref name="S1HomeMedia" /> the second season on September 6, 2016,<ref name="S2HomeMedia" /> the third season on September 5, 2017,<ref name="S3HomeMedia" /> the fourth season on August 28, 2018,<ref name="S4HomeMedia" /> and the fifth season on August 27, 2019.<ref name="S5HomeMedia" /> Each season release contains additional features, which include: making-of featurettes, episode commentaries, deleted scenes, gag reels, and Comic-Con panels.<ref name="S1HomeMedia" /> The second, fourth and fifth season boxsets include the Arrowverse crossover episodes from the other connected television series, as well as commentary on those episodes.<ref name="S2HomeMedia" /><ref name="S4DVDBestBuy" /><ref name="S5HomeMedia" /> On [[Netflix]] in the United States, the first season became available for streaming on October 6, 2015,<ref name="S1Netflix" /> the second season on October 4, 2016,<ref name="S2Netflix" /> the third season on May 31, 2017,<ref name="S3Netflix" /> the fourth season on May 30, 2018,<ref name="S4Netflix" /> and the fifth season on May 22, 2019.<ref name="S5Netflix" /> In India, the series streamed on [[Hotstar]] due to a partnership between them and [[Hooq]]; it dissolved in April 2020 after Hooq shut down due to bankruptcy. The last episode of the series to stream on Hotstar was "[[Death of the Speed Force]]" in mid-March.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 30, 2020 |title=Indian Arrowverse Fans Left in the Dark as Hooq Shuts Down |url=https://gadgets.ndtv.com/entertainment/news/the-flash-supergirl-legends-of-tomorrow-hooq-shut-down-hotstar-gone-india-watch-online-2220871 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200430074816/https://gadgets.ndtv.com/entertainment/news/the-flash-supergirl-legends-of-tomorrow-hooq-shut-down-hotstar-gone-india-watch-online-2220871 |archive-date=April 30, 2020 |access-date=April 30, 2020 |website=NDTV |language=en}}</ref> And in the same year, [[StarzPlay]] also acquired the series' streaming rights in [[Middle East]] & [[North Africa]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Watch "The Flash" on StarzPlay|url=https://starzplay.com/en/series/the-flash/46769704088|work=[[StarzPlay]]|accessdate=18 June 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=STARZPLAY viewership data reveals popularity of DC series in GCC|url=https://campaignme.com/starzplay-viewership-data-reveals-popularity-of-dc-series-in-gcc/|work=CampaignME|accessdate=28 June 2020}}</ref> A year later, [[Amazon Prime Video]] acquired the streaming rights for India, with the series premiering there on May 24, 2021.<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1395990486247038985 |user=PrimeVideoIN |title=a power storm is coming, we hope you are ready for these 👊 |author=amazon prime video IN |author-link=Amazon Prime Video |date=May 22, 2021 |access-date=May 22, 2021}}</ref>

